{"availability":"available","full":"MaxWinterstein/datetimetab","name":"datetimetab","description":"Today's date and time, live in your browser tab title. No extension, just a bookmark.","category":"Other","language":"JavaScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-29T20:34:28Z","updated":"2026-10-17T20:27:22Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://maxwinterstein.github.io/datetimetab/","topics":["clock","date","github-pages","pwa","tab-title","vanilla-js"],"demo":"https://maxwinterstein.github.io/datetimetab/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T22:41:09.799Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-17T22:41:09.799Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-17T22:41:09.799Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T19:51:15.009Z","stars":0,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-17T22:41:09.799Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Today's date and time in your browser tab title, live, in whatever format you like. No extension, just a bookmark.","Clock websites put only the time in the tab title; the few tools that show the date too are browser extensions. datetime-tab is a plain web page: pick a format, and the link carries it, so a bookmark or pinned tab is the whole setup, and you can send it to someone else. Pinned tabs hide the title, so the tab icon shows today's date as well."],"features":["Live tab title — document.title updates on each second boundary (or","each minute, if your format has no seconds), so it does not drift.","Presets or your own format — weekday clock, time only, ISO 8601, locale","default, date only, week number, Unix timestamp; or write a pattern with tokens (HH:mm:ss, ddd D MMM, …). The page shows a large live preview and a mock tab strip, so you can see where a long title gets cut off.","Language, time zone, 12/24 h, seconds, prefix/suffix — all adjustable."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MaxWinterstein/datetimetab/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"09bcce494ba9556d41e5f30a85dee9e0e9e444